            <div xml:id="d17">

                <pb facs="0101" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg16"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.00/1541</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gonzales</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Habana</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-04-16"><hi rendition="#italic">April 16,
                            1919</hi></date>.<lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received April
                        22.</hi>]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 989</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the Department’s instruction
                    No. 775 of March 24,<anchor n="11" xml:id="d17-f1"/> and to my despatch No. 985,
                    of April 15, in reply thereto, I have the honor to report that in President
                    Menocal’s semi-annual message to the Congress, published some days ago, I find
                    the following reference to the official work of General Crowder: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I intend to address in due course a special message to Congress
                            containing the report which I have requested of the illustrious <pb
                                facs="0102" n="17" xml:id="pg17"/> ex-President of the Advisory
                            Commission of 1906, who in large part drafted the electoral law now in
                            force, Major General E. H. Crowder, U.S. Army, and who by authorization
                            of his Government and at my invitation, is at present in this capital
                            engaged in this important task, and I am sure that his exceptional
                            ability in this line of work will be very helpful for the
                            recommendations which I shall at the proper time submit to the
                            consideration and resolution of Congress.[”]</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William E.
                    Gonzales</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
